WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.749
(slow mesmerizing music)

00:00:02.749 --> 00:00:05.477
- [Narrator] This is a
Louisiana pine snake.

00:00:05.477 --> 00:00:06.789
If we didn't show it to you,

00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:08.357
you'd probably never see it,

00:00:08.357 --> 00:00:11.944
or its distinct yellow and
brown splotched scales.

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:15.703
It's one of the rarest
snakes in North America.

00:00:15.703 --> 00:00:19.081
The Louisiana Pine Snake can
grow up to six feet long.

00:00:19.081 --> 00:00:21.995
It's not poisonous, it's a constrictor.

00:00:21.995 --> 00:00:24.700
It wraps its prey and suffocates them.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.105
Its known habitat is estimated

00:00:26.105 --> 00:00:28.177
to be less than 200 square miles

00:00:28.177 --> 00:00:32.601
in the Longleaf Pine forests
on the Louisiana-Texas border.

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:36.754
A habitat that is only 3%
of its once massive size.

00:00:36.754 --> 00:00:39.099
It's almost impossible to spot in the wild

00:00:39.099 --> 00:00:41.488
because the snake spends
its time below ground

00:00:41.488 --> 00:00:45.075
moving through the tunnels
of its rodent prey.

00:00:45.075 --> 00:00:46.968
But when it pokes its head above ground,

00:00:46.968 --> 00:00:49.928
its habitat is in steady decline.

00:00:49.928 --> 00:00:52.796
Fewer trees mean fewer
rodents burrowing beneath,

00:00:52.796 --> 00:00:55.106
and that means fewer pine snakes.

00:00:55.106 --> 00:00:56.964
And with encroaching human development,

00:00:56.964 --> 00:00:58.659
the ever present threat of being crushed

00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:00.958
under road traffic looms.

00:01:00.958 --> 00:01:03.315
This is Louisiana pine snake.

00:01:03.315 --> 00:01:06.732
(slow mesmerizing music)

